title: Eight Chairs
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

There are only eight [[Oka Chair]]s. [[Justin Beitzel]] told me so this month. Eight chairs. Each an argument you can sit in.

!! You can read it

The legs are thick because the wood is salvaged city timber with knots and insect damage in it, and the parts are wide enough that nobody worries about a stray knot. The hemp cord under the seat is left showing. The wool is undyed.

You can look at the chair and tell where it came from. [[The object is the visible result of a way of working]].

!! It is a sign

I have a name for this: [[Signs Signaling Sustainability]]. Sure, the chair is furniture, but it is also visual communication. Every exposed part says the same few things. Local. Salvaged. Able to go back. [[Circular|Circular Economy]]

!! The part that isn't true yet

The cord says local too. It wanted to be, however, currently it isn't.

Justin started with [[hemp|Hemp]] from a farm in western Maryland and tried making the rope himself: retting, hackling, weaving. A few feet took so long that the 300 feet one seat needs was out of reach (let's make a chair a quarter!). Then he found a rope maker in North Carolina working with American fiber. The plan was to send them fiber as orders grew, and one day to buy machines to then do it back here in Baltimore.

Three years later that rope maker has closed. The fiber comes from France for now, so it could still be single origin traced. This is currently where the chair's [[BioRegion]] breaks.

!! What is missing

Nobody forgot how to turn hemp into rope. Justin did it by hand. A Maryland farm grew the plant.

What is gone is the middle. The U.S. has almost no fiber processing left, and a small studio can't rebuild it alone. The wool shows the difference: it works because [[Melissa Smith]] has decided to run a wool mill on her own farm.

[[We used to know how]]. We also used to have the machines.

!! An honest sign

A juice carton says "recyclable." But! only about one in five gets recycled ([[Tetra Pak]]). The recycling arrows mean four different things ([[Rethinking the Recycling Symbol]]). Those signs are true, I mean sort of, but they mislead, or are maybe just completely misunderstood?

The Oka Chair doesn't have something as "simple" as a recycling symbol. The hope is that its form and materials say "local." Ask the person who makes it, and he tells you which part isn't, and why. The carton hides its gap behind a true word. The chair's maker names his. I trust it more for that. A sign that shows its gap. A maker honest about the good and the bad.

[[Does a sign become more honest when its maker admits what it doesn't yet do?]] Even if the label doesn't?

!! Eight, then more

How does eight become more? By [[Scaling Out]]: more farms and more hubs. A Michigan grant is hopefully paying to test wool from farms there. About five are lined up. Someone in Toronto wants to make and sell the chair there.

Each hub is a small distributed factory with its own supply chain. This is bioregionalism at work.

Justin's test for "enough" is the point where the impact tips from regenerative to degrading. He says that is a long way off.

For now: eight chairs, and French hemp.

